(FIL-35: opening at a numeric literal borrows that file descriptor rather than treating the number as a path.
 Also covers: FIL-36, FIL-42.
 The final guard proves the negative: no file named "2" was created, so
 at 2 was not read as a path. fd 1 is deliberately NOT borrowed here -
 Close on a borrowed fd 1 is a real close(2) and would take the
 program's own stdout with it.
 Run from the vox-fuzz worktree root; the probe creates everything it
 needs under vf_scratch/files/ and touches nothing else.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox docs/ledger/probes/files/FIL-35.vox -o p && ./p
 actual output:
   2
   1
   0
   0
   1
   no file named 2 was created, so at 2 was a borrowed descriptor
)
open a file for writing called 'the standard error' at 2.
print 'the standard error's descriptor.
print 'the standard error's writable.
print 'the standard error's readable.
open a file for reading called 'the standard input' at 0.
print 'the standard input's descriptor.
print 'the standard input's readable.
print "no file named 2 was created, so at 2 was a borrowed descriptor".
If "2" is available then,
    print "a file called 2 exists - at 2 was treated as a path".
